<!-- Phase 9 website teaching layer; original chapter remains read-only. Evidence: website/reviews/android-phase9-findings.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A new optional parameter looks harmless because every source call still compiles. An older compiled consumer fails at runtime. “Compatible” needs an object: source, binary, generated Swift API, serialized data or behaviour. Each promise needs its own evidence.</p>

<!-- @after Compatibility Is Multidimensional :: visual -->
### One change, different witnesses

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="2"><div class="node"><strong>Source consumer</strong><small>Recompile the existing call site.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Compiled consumer</strong><small>Exercise the old caller with the candidate library where binary replacement is promised.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Data contract</strong><small>Read golden and mixed-version payloads.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Behaviour and export</strong><small>Check lifecycle/error semantics and the generated Swift API.</small></div></div><figcaption>An ABI dump is one witness. It cannot prove every promise listed in the original compatibility table.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after High-Risk Kotlin Changes :: animation -->
### Why recompilation can hide a binary break

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">An existing caller asks for the old entry point</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="old" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Previously compiled caller</strong><small data-text="oldV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="candidate" data-art="parcel" data-art-role="queue"><strong>Candidate library</strong><small data-text="candidateV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="source" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Recompiled source fixture</strong><small data-text="sourceV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="gate" data-art="gate" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Compatibility gate</strong><small data-text="gateV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{
"label": "Before change",
"caption": "The compiled caller references the original JVM method descriptor.",
"state": {
"old": "active"
},
"text": {
"oldV": "fetchUser(String)",
"candidateV": "original entry point",
"sourceV": "existing source call",
"gateV": "baseline"
},
"add": [
"old",
"candidate",
"source",
"gate"
]
},
{
"label": "Replace signature",
"caption": "Suppose the library replaces it with a signature that adds a Boolean parameter.",
"state": {
"candidate": "warn"
},
"text": {
"candidateV": "fetchUser(String, Boolean)"
}
},
{
"label": "Recompile source",
"caption": "A default value can satisfy the source call during recompilation. That does not rewrite the old compiled caller.",
"state": {
"source": "done",
"old": "warn"
},
"text": {
"sourceV": "source fixture can compile",
"oldV": "still references old descriptor"
}
},
{
"label": "Exercise the promise",
"caption": "ABI review and an old-consumer test expose the missing entry point. Preserve a deliberate compatible path or version the break.",
"state": {
"gate": "active"
},
"text": {
"gateV": "review + old consumer/new library"
}
}
]</script><figcaption>This traces the JVM example. Native, Swift, serialization and behaviour need their own gates rather than an assumed identical repair.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Release Policy and Evidence :: check -->
### Try the decision

<details><summary>checkKotlinAbi is green. Can retry semantics change silently?</summary>

The dump does not establish behavioural compatibility. Test the timeout, replay, ordering and cancellation contract promised to callers.
</details>

<details><summary>Why retain an old compiled consumer fixture?</summary>

Recompiling uses the new API and can hide missing old entry points. The old artifact exercises the binary promise directly.
</details>

<details><summary>A new server enum value is absent from old clients. Is this only an API-dump concern?</summary>

No. It is also a wire/rollout and safe-behaviour decision. Test old/new readers and consumers under the declared compatibility window.
</details>
